        "title": "APACHE VS. NAVAJO (DINÉ): A Tale of Two Cousin Nations The Apache and Navajo peoples share a deep ancestral bond, both speaking Athabascan languages—though Apaches tend to speak at a quicker pace. Their tongues are related, yet their traditions, lifestyles, and styles carry striking differences. Both were once fierce raiders, but the Navajo shifted away from raiding far earlier. Even their headbands tell different stories—the Navajo favor a 3-inch band knotted to the side (usually right), while the Apache wear wider bands, 4–8 inches, tied at the back. Geronimo himself famously wrapped his like a pirate, covering the entire head. Footwear carried its own signature. Navajo moccasins were often adorned with metal buttons or accents, while Apache moccasins were purely practical—buckskin with an upturned tip to guard against sharp rocks, cactus spines, and stumbles during a run. Apache women wore their hair loose like the men, while Navajo women tied it into a traditional rear bun. Dresses, too, were distinct—Apache women became known for the timeless camp dress, still beloved today. The Apache were unmatched runners, covering vast distances on foot—sometimes even. Opens in new tab.",
